      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Ornamental pot olive vs orchard tree (starter row 63). Watering: RHS says water container plants regularly from April to late September, barely moist over winter, pots on feet; established ground trees are drought tolerant; NC State drought tolerant once established, no frequency. let_top_dry is the agent's mapping, interval 5 days a guess. Pots: at least 30 to 35 cm wide and deep, frost-proof, soil-based peat-free compost with about 20 percent grit; pinch out tips at about 1.5 m to keep a pot tree compact. Light: both full sun. Temperature: RHS says many olives tolerate -5 to -10 C and severe frost (below -5 C) can still damage them; the milder end is stored as the minimum; container plants need winter protection. NC State USDA zones 8a to 10b (7 for some cultivars) and needs two months at 4 to 10 C to flower. Size: NC State 20 to 30 ft by 15 to 25 ft (upper values, an in-ground tree); a pot tree stays far smaller, hence from_knowledge_verify; RHS gives no size. Growth slow (RHS and NC State agree). Habit upright is a mapping of 'erect, rounded'. Toxicity UNKNOWN for pets and humans: neither fetched page says anything and the ASPCA searches found only Elaeagnus ('Russian olive'), a different plant; the fruit is edible after preparation (RHS, NC State). On the pets-unknown list. Xylella fastidiosa: RHS says olives are a high-risk host (relevant to buying plants). Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
